{0 Find references (backlinks)}

Find all locations in the vault that link {e to} a given file or
heading — the inverse of go-to-definition.

{1 Input}

A cursor position (0-based line and character) in a markdown file that
belongs to a vault with a built index.

{1 Activation}

The request is [textDocument/references].  The server advertises
[referencesProvider] in its capabilities.

Two activation modes:

{ol
  {- {b Cursor on a link}: find all other references to the same
     resolved target.}
  {- {b Cursor on an anchor's line}: find all links that target that
     specific anchor in this file. Both anchor kinds count — heading and
     attribute id — including an inline [ [text]\{#id\} ].}
}

The anchors come from {!Lsp_lib.Anchors}, i.e. from the parser: a [#] inside
a fenced code block is not an anchor, and a heading's identity is the id the
parser assigned rather than one re-derived from its text.
See {!page-"feature-index"}.

A block attribute is reached from either the [ \{#id\} ] line or the block
it attributes.

If the cursor is on neither a link nor an anchor, no results are returned.

{1 Collection}

{2 Step 1 — Determine the reference target}

{ul
  {- If the cursor is on a link, resolve it via
     {!Oystermark.Vault.Index.resolve} to obtain the target path
     (and optional fragment).}
  {- If the cursor is on a heading, the target is (current file,
     heading slug).}
  {- If the cursor is on a block ID, the target is (current file,
     block ID).}
}

{2 Step 2 — Scan the vault}

Walk every indexed document and collect links whose resolution matches
the reference target:

{ul
  {- {b Path-only target}: collect links that resolve to the same
     file, regardless of fragment.}
  {- {b Path + fragment target}: collect links that resolve to the
     same file {e and} whose fragment matches.}
}

{1:generated Generated links are tagged, not dropped}

A link inside a [::: toc] region (see {!page-"feature-toc"}) was written by
the server, not by the author.  Every reference carries an [in_toc] flag
saying so, set during the same fold that collects it, with the region
recognized by the TOC feature's own predicate — so there is one definition
of what a region is, and a [::: toc] inside a code block is not one here
either.

It is a flag rather than a filter because the callers want opposite things
from it:

{t
  | Caller | Generated links |
  |--------|-----------------|
  | [textDocument/references] | included — the link is really there, and the writer asked to see every one |
  | {!page-"feature-rename"} | rewritten — skipping them would leave the TOC stale the moment a heading is renamed |
  | {!page-"feature-codelens-reference-counts"} | not counted, unless [codeLens.countTocLinks] says otherwise |
}

A flag also leaves that last row a {e setting} rather than a fork in the
scan: both answers are one [List.filter] away from the same result, and
the scan does not have to know which one the vault asked for.

{1 Result}

A list of LSP [Location] objects, one per referring link.  Each
location covers the full byte range of the link syntax (brackets
included).

{t
  | Field | Value                              |
  |-------|------------------------------------|
  | uri   | file URI of the referring document |
  | range | byte range of the link             |
}

Results are sorted by (uri, range.start) for determinism.

{1 Edge cases}

{ul
  {- {b Unresolved link under cursor}: no results (cannot determine
     target).}
  {- {b No references found}: empty list.}
  {- {b Self-references}: a document linking to itself is included
     in results.}
  {- {b Embed links} ([![[note]]]): included as references, same as
     regular wikilinks.}
}

{1 Assumptions and limitations}

{ul
  {- Scanning all indexed documents on every request.  For large
     vaults, a pre-built reverse index may be needed.}
  {- Only links in the vault index are considered; files outside the
     vault root are not scanned.}
}
